# Changing files safely

A complete generation plan is atomically valid in memory: either every planned
file is available or none is. A filesystem cannot usually replace several files at
one physical instant. Between those two facts lies the
[output transaction](term:output-transaction).

The transaction's purpose is narrower than the database term may suggest. It gives
a batch recoverable behavior for caught failures: validate before mutation, stage
all changed bytes, retain originals while installing replacements, and attempt to
restore the old state if preparation or installation fails. It does not turn an
ordinary filesystem into a crash-durable, isolated database.

## Safety begins by naming the promises

For one serialized writer and a cooperating filesystem adapter, the transaction
maintains these invariants:

1. unchanged outputs cause no file or directory changes;
2. every destination and parent is checked before mutation begins;
3. all changed content is staged before any target is replaced;
4. an original remains recoverable until the whole batch reaches its commit point;
5. rollback removes newly installed outputs and restores originals in reverse order;
6. cleanup removes only artifacts the transaction knows it owns;
7. every failure reports whether the requested bytes were committed.

These promises make many ordinary errors recoverable without overstating atomicity.
The distinction matters because callers choose very different actions after “nothing
was committed,” “rollback was incomplete,” and “the files were committed but cleanup
failed.”

## Preflight the complete destination set

Preflight validates the batch before creating even an empty directory. Paths are
normalized root-relative names. Case-folded duplicates are rejected for portability,
as are conflicts where one output would also be another output's parent. Input byte
arrays are copied so a caller cannot change planned content while asynchronous work
is in progress.

The filesystem adapter then inspects every parent and target, including paths for
outputs whose bytes may prove unchanged. Existing ancestors must be directories.
Existing targets must be regular files. Symbolic links, special entries, and errors
other than genuine absence reject the batch.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Batch[Planned output batch] --> Normalize[Normalize paths and copy bytes]
    Normalize --> Conflicts{Any path conflict?}
    Conflicts -->|Yes| Reject[Reject without mutation]
    Conflicts -->|No| Walk[Inspect all parents and targets]
    Walk --> Safe{Every path acceptable?}
    Safe -->|No| Reject
    Safe -->|Yes| Compare[Read and compare existing bytes]
    Compare --> Ready[Changed set ready]
```

The arrows show permission being earned. They do not imply that the filesystem is
frozen between checks. That remaining race is one reason the caller must serialize
writers and exclude unrelated changes during a batch.

Preflight also reserves private staging names without creating them. One staging
directory is chosen within each changed target's parent. Keeping stage, backup, and
target on the same filesystem and near the same directory avoids cross-filesystem
moves and limits the scope of temporary artifacts.

## Preparation is visible but not published

Preparation creates only missing parent directories required by changed outputs,
then exclusively creates each private staging directory and stage file. Exclusive
creation is essential: encountering another entry is a collision, not permission to
reuse or erase it.

Every changed file is written completely to its stage before installation begins.
At this point the filesystem contains private preparation material, but all public
targets still have their original meaning.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant T as Transaction
    participant FS as Filesystem adapter

    T->>FS: Create owned missing parents
    T->>FS: Create private staging directories
    loop Every changed output
        T->>FS: Write complete stage exclusively
    end
    Note over T,FS: Public targets are still untouched
```

Ownership must be based on positive knowledge. If exclusive directory creation
fails because another writer won a race, the transaction must not infer that the
observed directory belongs to it. Only a successful creation, or a precise adapter
error saying that this operation created the directory but could not compensate,
transfers cleanup responsibility to the coordinator.

## Installation keeps originals nearby

Installation handles each changed output in plan order. If a target already exists,
it is renamed to the private backup location. The stage is then renamed to the
public target. For a new output there is no backup; the staged file simply becomes
the target.

The transaction records an operation as attempted *before* asking the adapter to
rename. Some filesystem interfaces can report failure after the move has taken
effect. A rejected operation therefore means “the result is uncertain,” not always
“nothing happened.” Recovery inspects both source and destination rather than
trusting the shape of an exception.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Preflight
    Preflight --> Prepared: all stages written
    Prepared --> Installing
    Installing --> Installed: every target replaced
    Installed --> Committed: final cancellation check passes
    Preflight --> Rejected: validation fails
    Prepared --> Restoring: preparation fails
    Installing --> Restoring: installation or cancellation fails
    Restoring --> RolledBack: restoration succeeds
    Restoring --> RecoveryNeeded: restoration is uncertain
    Committed --> Complete: cleanup succeeds
    Committed --> CleanupNeeded: cleanup fails
```

The state diagram separates three facts that an undifferentiated error would hide:
whether public installation began, whether restoration succeeded, and whether the
commit point had already passed.

## The commit point comes before cleanup

The batch commits only after every changed target has been installed and the final
pre-commit cancellation check passes. Until then, retained backups support rollback.
After that point the requested output bytes are authoritative.

Cleanup follows commitment. It removes backup and stage files, then removes empty
private directories. Deleting a backup is irreversible, so cleanup failure cannot
honestly turn a committed batch back into an uncommitted one. The result must say
“committed, cleanup failed” and report the remaining recovery locations.

Cancellation follows the same rule. Before commitment it can trigger rollback.
After commitment it cannot promise to undo already published files, so no new
cancellation checkpoint interrupts cleanup.

`transaction commit` keeps the truthful outcome visible in one bounded routine.

```typescript: <<transaction commit>>=
export async function commitOutputs(files: readonly TransactionFile[], io: TransactionIO,
    checkCancelled?: () => void): Promise<string[]> {
    const state = await preflight(files, io, checkCancelled);
    try {
        await prepareOutputs(state, io);
        checkCancelled?.();
        await installOutputs(state, io);
        checkCancelled?.();
    } catch (error) {
        let failures = await rollbackOutputs(state, io);
        if (!failures.length) { failures = await cleanupTransaction(state, io, true); }
        throw new OutputTransactionError(failures.length ? 'rollback_failed' : 'rolled_back', false,
            [...new Set([state.active, ...failures.map(failure => failure.path)])],
            failures.length ? [...state.owned, ...state.parents] : [], error,
            failures.map(failure => `${failure.path}: ${String(failure.error)}`));
    }
    const failures = await cleanupTransaction(state, io, false);
    if (failures.length) {
        throw new OutputTransactionError('cleanup_failed', true, failures.map(failure => failure.path),
            state.owned, failures[0].error, failures.map(failure => `${failure.path}: ${String(failure.error)}`),
            state.outputs.map(output => output.path));
    }
    return state.outputs.map(output => output.path);
}
```

## Rollback reasons from observed state

When preparation or installation fails, rollback visits changed outputs in reverse
order. Reverse order unwinds dependencies introduced by the forward sequence and
reduces the number of mixed states that recovery must consider.

For a replaced output, recovery first establishes that the original backup still
exists. It must not delete an installed target and only then discover that the
backup is gone. If a stage remains beside a public target after an uncertain rename,
the situation is ambiguous; preserving both is safer than guessing which bytes to
destroy.

A successful rollback restores every original, removes every new target, removes
owned stage and backup files, and removes newly created parents only when they are
empty. Cleanup is nonrecursive. Unexpected content causes a reported failure rather
than broad deletion.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Failure[Preparation or installation failure] --> Reverse[Visit outputs in reverse]
    Reverse --> Certain{State safe to undo?}
    Certain -->|Yes| Restore[Remove new target or restore backup]
    Certain -->|No| Preserve[Preserve recovery material]
    Restore --> More{More outputs?}
    More -->|Yes| Reverse
    More -->|No| Clean[Remove known artifacts and empty owned parents]
    Preserve --> Report[Report recovery paths]
    Clean --> Outcome{Cleanup complete?}
    Outcome -->|Yes| Rolled[Report rolled back]
    Outcome -->|No| Report
```

The preservation branch is deliberate. Recovery code has more power to destroy
valuable evidence than the failed installation had. When ownership or state is
uncertain, fail closed and tell the operator where to inspect.

## Failure results describe what is true

The transaction distinguishes four outcomes rather than flattening them into
“write failed”:

- **Preflight failed.** No mutation was attempted. The old filesystem state remains
  outside the transaction's ownership.
- **Rolled back.** Mutation began, but originals and absence of new outputs were
  restored and owned artifacts were removed.
- **Rollback failed.** Restoration or its cleanup could not be completed. Public
  targets may be mixed, and reported recovery material must be preserved for
  inspection.
- **Cleanup failed after commit.** Every requested output was installed, but some
  owned stage or backup material remains. Retrying the whole generation blindly
  would misrepresent what already succeeded.

A failure also identifies the active or conflicting paths, the initiating error,
and any retained recovery locations. For a committed cleanup failure it includes
the changed output list that a successful transaction would have returned. This
allows a host to report installed files without pretending cleanup succeeded.

Recovery locations are evidence, not rubbish. They may contain the only surviving
original after an interrupted restoration. A person or recovery tool should inspect
the reported target, stage, and backup states before moving or deleting anything.

```NOTE
An untidy recovery directory can be good news: it may be the transaction refusing
to destroy the last trustworthy copy merely to leave a neat workspace behind.
```

## Filesystem adapters carry the physical contract

The coordinator is independent of a particular filesystem API. Its adapter must
provide path inspection, reads, exclusive single-directory creation, exclusive file
creation, non-overwriting rename, nonrecursive removal, and a safe unique token.
Those operations are obligations, not assumptions about a platform's default calls.

Inspection validates every existing component and the trusted root. Rename must not
silently overwrite its destination. Removal checks the expected kind again. If the
platform cannot provide a genuinely non-overwriting rename, an inspection followed
by rename narrows ordinary mistakes but leaves a race with another writer. The
serialization contract remains necessary.

A local adapter can preserve ordinary permission bits by remembering them when it
reads an original and applying them to the replacement stage before installation.
A remote editor provider may not expose portable permission metadata. Sharing the
transaction protocol does not erase such physical differences.

## What atomicity does not mean here

The transaction does **not** promise simultaneous visibility. A reader that ignores
the serialization boundary may observe some new files while later files still hold
old content. It does not maintain a durable journal, so a process crash or power
loss can stop between any two filesystem operations. It does not lock out another
writer, and preflight cannot eliminate time-of-check-to-time-of-use races.

It also does not delete obsolete outputs absent from the new plan. The plan can say
what should be produced from the current snapshot; it cannot prove that an unrelated
old file is safe to remove.

The honest claim is therefore precise: for caught failures in a serialized batch,
retain enough information to restore the prior state, attempt that restoration
conservatively, and report uncertainty without destroying recovery material. This
is strong enough to protect ordinary generation while leaving crash durability,
concurrent isolation, and global atomic visibility to systems designed to provide
them.

Safe commitment protects the products of a generator. It does not yet answer a more
circular question: how can the generator trust a new copy of itself when that copy
is one of its own products? The next chapter replaces filesystem recovery with a
fixed-point argument over bootstrap material and freshly generated code.
